questions.body:
Why does the following code fail to compile?
```java
try {
    int result = 10 / 0;
} catch (Exception e) {
    System.out.println("Exception");
} catch (ArithmeticException e) {
    System.out.println("Arithmetic");
}
```

What is printed by executing this snippet?
```java
try {
    System.out.print("A");
    throw new IllegalArgumentException();
} catch (IllegalArgumentException e) {
    System.out.print("B");
} finally {
    System.out.print("C");
}
```

Consider the following code:
```java
static void inner() throws IOException {
    throw new IOException("Disk full");
}
static void outer() throws IOException {
    inner();
}
```
Why does `outer()` compile without a try-catch block?

What value does this method return?
```java
static int calculate() {
    try {
        return 10;
    } finally {
        return 20;
    }
}
```

Given multiple resources declared in a try-with-resources header:
```java
try (ResourceA a = new ResourceA(); ResourceB b = new ResourceB()) {
    // work
}
```
In what order are the resources closed?

Consider the following method:
```java
static void execute() throws IOException, SQLException {
    try {
        riskyOperation();
    } catch (final Exception e) {
        throw e;
    }
}
```
If `riskyOperation()` only throws `IOException` and `SQLException`, why does this compile without declaring `throws Exception`?

Given that `FileNotFoundException` extends `IOException`, what is printed by the following code?
```java
try {
    throw new FileNotFoundException();
} catch (IOException e) {
    System.out.println("Caught: " + e.getClass().getSimpleName());
}
```

Why is the following pattern considered a severe software anti-pattern?
```java
try {
    processPayment(order);
} catch (Exception e) {
    // do nothing
}
```
